From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 08:40:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: cpu-locks: survive registers a core
 lacks; boot lines off

The 1.924 trial died on both tries before journald started: an oops on
CPU 0, ESR.EC 0x0 (undefined instruction), at cl_read_this_cpu+0x290,
the read of EHID4, which that core does not implement. The read has an
exception table entry, but do_el1_undef() never looked at the table:
the fixup this driver relied on was in the 1.922 base and is not in
this one.

Apply the exception table fixup in do_el1_undef() before dying, as the
data abort path does, so a register a core lacks reads as "-" instead
of killing the boot. And print the boot and resume lines only with
apple_cpu_locks.log=1 until a boot with them on has been seen to pass;
debugfs apple_cpu_locks still reads on demand.

Fixes: 727c156595ed ("soc: apple: print the CPU range locks at boot and after each sleep")
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: LLM
---
 arch/arm64/kernel/traps.c     | 3 +++
 drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig     | 2 +-
 drivers/soc/apple/cpu-locks.c | 9 +++++----
 3 files changed, 9 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/kernel/traps.c b/arch/arm64/kernel/traps.c
index e02cc6c38b..778ef41868 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/traps.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/traps.c
@@ -485,6 +485,9 @@ void do_el1_undef(struct pt_regs *regs, unsigned long esr)
 		return;
 
 out_err:
+	/* A fault-safe access with an exception table entry (an IMP DEF sysreg probe). */
+	if (fixup_exception(regs, esr))
+		return;
 	die("Oops - Undefined instruction", regs, esr);
 }
 
diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig b/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig
index f6f38f5a43..68511741e3 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig
@@ -270,7 +270,7 @@ config APPLE_CPU_LOCKS
 	  kernel image lies in a write lock. A core back from a deep sleep
 	  takes its locks again at reset, so they can differ from boot.
 	  Reads only; debugfs apple_cpu_locks gives the same on demand.
-	  apple_cpu_locks.log=0 silences the lines.
+	  The boot and resume lines need apple_cpu_locks.log=1.
 
 config APPLE_BOOT_CRUMB
 	bool "Apple boot crumbs in PMU scratch"
diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/cpu-locks.c b/drivers/soc/apple/cpu-locks.c
index 66d6520ae3..07b2ae2165 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/cpu-locks.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/cpu-locks.c
@@ -10,8 +10,9 @@
  *
  * This prints one line per online CPU at boot and after each resume: the
  * locks, the cluster copies, SPRR/GXF and HID4/EHID4, and whether the kernel
- * image's text or data lies in a write lock. debugfs apple_cpu_locks gives the
- * same on demand. Reads only, without loading a module or patching text, so it
+ * image's text or data lies in a write lock, when apple_cpu_locks.log=1.
+ * debugfs apple_cpu_locks gives the same on demand. Reads only, without loading
+ * a module or patching text, so it
  * can be read on a resumed system that must not be disturbed. Each register is
  * read fault-safe: one a core lacks shows as "-".
  */
@@ -37,9 +38,9 @@
 #undef MODULE_PARAM_PREFIX
 #define MODULE_PARAM_PREFIX "apple_cpu_locks."
 
-static bool log_lines = true;
+static bool log_lines;
 module_param_named(log, log_lines, bool, 0644);
-MODULE_PARM_DESC(log, "print the per-CPU lines at boot and after each resume");
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(log, "print the per-CPU lines at boot and after each resume (default off)");
 
 #define CL_BAD	(~0ULL)
 
-- 
2.43.0

